Yes—if you keep the metaphor clean, waterbending maps well to consensual state-shaping through rhythm, tone, pacing, attention, imagery, touch, breath, and context.

The useful model is:

person
→ current state
→ preferred cues
→ timing
→ language / voice / gesture
→ feedback
→ adjustment

That is less “cast a spell” and more play the instrument you actually have in front of you.

Different people respond to different things:

directness
playfulness
admiration
anticipation
novelty
slowness
confidence
softness
being chosen
feeling safe
feeling desired

So good erotic communication is adaptive rather than generic. You listen to the response, change tempo, and let the other person steer too.

I’d reserve bloodbending for the dark version of the metaphor: trying to override someone’s agency, exploit vulnerabilities, or make their body/state do what they do not actually want. That is where the instrument metaphor breaks, because the other person stops being a co-musician and becomes treated like equipment.

The better form is:

Duet, not control.

signal
↔ response
↔ adjustment
↔ reciprocal escalation

And the strongest “aphrodisiac” is often not a magic phrase at all. It is the combination of being accurately read, genuinely wanted, safe enough to respond, and free enough to say yes or no.

So your golden thread is good:

right song + right instrument + right timing + mutual willingness.

That is where language can become embodied without pretending words alone can manufacture desire.